<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  :data owns the manifest-declared platform entry points (LLM.md §3.6): the notification listener
  service, the push service and the receivers. They are declared here, beside their code, and merge
  into :app. None exists yet — their clusters have not been written.

  PERMISSIONS: only what code in THIS module actually calls. The competitor declares 35
  <uses-permission> entries, 13 of which it never uses and two of which are not real AOSP names
  (docs/reverse-engineering/02 §1). A permission a store listing has to justify and no code reads is
  a review risk with no feature behind it.

  What is deliberately NOT here:
  * SYSTEM_ALERT_WINDOW, RECEIVE_BOOT_COMPLETED, POST_NOTIFICATIONS — each sits behind a pending
    owner decision (App Lock overlay, out-of-app assertiveness). Nothing here declares a permission
    on a decision's behalf. PACKAGE_USAGE_STATS was on this list and no longer is; its own block
    below states exactly what that line settles and what it leaves open.
  * ACCESS_MEDIA_LOCATION — MediaStoreQuery reads no EXIF location; the photo cluster declares it if
    its own code does.
-->
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">

    <!--
      Read access for MediaStoreQuery / DefaultMediaStoreRepository — strategy 3 of §8.3.
      API 33 split one permission into three; below 33 READ_EXTERNAL_STORAGE is the only form, so it
      is capped rather than left to look like a live request on a modern device.
    -->
    <uses-permission
        android:name="android.permission.READ_EXTERNAL_STORAGE"
        android:maxSdkVersion="32" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.READ_MEDIA_IMAGES" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.READ_MEDIA_VIDEO" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.READ_MEDIA_AUDIO" />

    <!--
      DefaultFileDeleter only. minSdk is 28, and on API 28 deleting a file on shared storage needs
      the write permission; from API 29 scoped storage replaces it with MediaStore.createDeleteRequest
      (API 30+) and SAF, both of which need no manifest permission at all. Capped at 28 so it is
      requested on exactly the one API level that requires it.
    -->
    <uses-permission
        android:name="android.permission.WRITE_EXTERNAL_STORAGE"
        android:maxSdkVersion="28" />

    <!--
      ALL-FILES ACCESS. Declared by owner decision of 2026-09-06, which REVERSES the 2026-08-26 ruling
      that this permission is never assumed grantable (system-architecture §8.1). The reversal was made
      with the trade-off on the table, so do not re-litigate it: re-read this comment instead.

      WHY IT HAD TO BE DECLARED. The junk scan is a filesystem walk, not a MediaStore query. Without
      this grant `AndroidStorageRootProvider.readableRoots()` returns exactly three directories — the
      app's own cacheDir, externalCacheDir and filesDir — so all three passes walked this app's own
      sandbox and reported 0 B on a device that really holds hundreds of megabytes of stale `.apk`
      files. That is worse than a missing feature: the screen said "scanned, nothing found".
      A `.apk` belonging to another app is a NON-MEDIA file, so neither READ_MEDIA_* nor
      MediaStore.Files can see it, and since Android 11 ACTION_OPEN_DOCUMENT_TREE refuses the internal
      volume root and the Download directory. There is no third path.

      WHAT IT STILL DOES NOT BUY. Android/data and Android/obb stay unreadable on API 30+ even holding
      this permission (gioi-han-android §1) — which is where a modern app's cache actually lives, and it
      is the single biggest bucket on any real device. An app-residual root that points there carries
      `AppRuleRoot.scoped = true` in JunkAppResidualRules.kt and is skipped knowably; the system-cache
      list simply has no rule for it, because there is nothing there we can size or delete.

      WHAT IT COSTS. Google Play reviews MANAGE_EXTERNAL_STORAGE against the core function declared and
      promoted on the store listing, not against the code. The declaration must be justified there
      before this build is submitted. Below API 30 there is no such permission: on API 28 the legacy
      read + write pair is the equivalent, and on API 29 scoped storage means there is no all-files
      access to have — `StorageAccessChecks.allFiles()` spells out both branches.

      tools:ignore="ScopedStorage" — lint flags every declaration of this permission on principle; the
      justification is the six paragraphs above rather than a suppression with no reason beside it.
    -->
    <uses-permission
        android:name="android.permission.MANAGE_EXTERNAL_STORAGE"
        tools:ignore="ScopedStorage" />

    <!--
      Read by `PackageManagerInstalledAppsRepository.lastUsedByPackage`
      (`UsageStatsManager.queryUsageStats`) and checked by `AndroidPermissionRepository.appOpAllowed`
      and `ForegroundAppResolver.hasUsageAccess` — code in THIS module, which is the rule every other
      line here follows.

      IT MUST BE DECLARED EVEN THOUGH NO DIALOG ASKS FOR IT. The user gives this grant by hand on the
      system Usage Access page, and that page lists ONLY apps whose manifest declares the permission.
      Without this line App Manager's "Open settings" button opened a page the app could never appear
      on: the grant was unreachable, `isGranted(UsageStats)` read false on every device, and the
      screen's rationale card pointed at a dead end.

      SCOPE. This closes the App Manager half of pending owner decision 3 — the *Last used* column and
      its sort chip, where `docs/screens/14` §5 already makes the grant optional and opens the screen
      without it. It settles nothing about the running-apps screen: that fork
      (`docs/system-architecture.md` §10.1 P1) is still open, nothing in `:feature:device` reads a
      usage statistic, and declaring the permission does not make it do so.

      tools:ignore="ProtectedPermissions" — the protection level is signature|privileged|appop and
      lint reads the first two without the third. `appop` is precisely what makes it user-grantable.
    -->
    <uses-permission
        android:name="android.permission.PACKAGE_USAGE_STATS"
        tools:ignore="ProtectedPermissions" />

    <!--
      PACKAGE VISIBILITY — without these two declarations App Manager lists TWO rows.

      `targetSdk` is 36, so the API 30 filtering applies in full: `PackageManager` answers
      `getInstalledPackages` and `queryIntentActivities` with only the packages this app is allowed
      to see, and says nothing about the rest. It is not an error and not an empty list — it is a
      shorter list, which is why `PackageManagerInstalledAppsRepository.enumerate` cannot detect it
      and no `SecurityException` arm fires. Measured on device RF8Y60B9NCZ (SM-A165F, Android 16):
      `dumpsys package com.pion.phonecleaner` reported `system apps queryable: false` with empty
      forceQueryable and package-name query lists, and the screen showed 2 apps where
      `pm list packages -3` counts 72. The competitor shows 71 on the same device — 72 minus itself —
      and it gets there with exactly the two declarations below (`decompiler/app-src/AndroidManifest.xml:107`
      and its `<queries>` block).

      BOTH are declared, because they cover different halves of the list and fail differently.

      `<queries>` is the half that needs no store justification. ACTION_MAIN + CATEGORY_LAUNCHER is
      the literal query `launchablePackages()` runs, so without it `includeWithoutLauncher = false`
      — the Permission Manager's enumeration — returns near-nothing on its own terms. Bare ACTION_MAIN
      widens that to apps whose entry activity carries no LAUNCHER category.

      QUERY_ALL_PACKAGES is the half that does. It is the only thing that reveals a user-installed
      package with no MAIN activity at all, which is precisely what `includeWithoutLauncher = true`
      — the App Manager — asks for; a keyboard, a plugin or a work-profile companion is invisible
      without it. Play treats it as sensitive and requires a declared use case; a device cleaner with
      an uninstall manager is one of the permitted ones. If it is ever stripped from a build, the
      `<queries>` block above still carries the launchable majority, so the screen degrades to
      "most apps" rather than back to two.
    -->
    <!-- The app-manager inventory above deliberately includes packages with no launcher;
         a queries intent cannot express that inventory. Keep this one declared exception visible. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.QUERY_ALL_PACKAGES"
        tools:ignore="QueryAllPackagesPermission" />

    <queries>
        <intent>
            <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
            <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
        </intent>
        <intent>
            <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
        </intent>
    </queries>

    <!--
      POST_NOTIFICATIONS is deliberately ABSENT. It sits behind a pending owner decision
      (out-of-app assertiveness), and `AppNotifier.areNotificationsEnabled()` already reports the
      answer: without the grant the id-900 summary no-ops, visibly, rather than silently claiming to
      have been checked. Nothing here declares a permission on a decision's behalf.
    -->

    <application>

        <!--
          The notification listener (docs/screens/17 §5). It replaces `DevitioService` + `od.i` + the
          `flux_refresh_noti_list` event channel.

          `BIND_NOTIFICATION_LISTENER_SERVICE` is a SIGNATURE permission held by the SYSTEM, not by
          this app: declaring it here states that only the system may bind this service. It is NOT a
          permission the user grants and is therefore not one of the pending owner decisions — the
          user instead enables notification access in a system Settings screen, which
          `NotificationGateRoute` opens and `PermissionRepository.isGranted(NotificationListener)`
          reads back.

          `exported` must be true — the system is an external binder — and that is safe precisely
          because the signature permission gates it. Contrast `mf.EnsileActivity`, exported with no
          intent-filter, no permission and no caller check (LLM.md §7.3).

          No `android:process`: a second process would need its own Koin start and its own Room
          connection to the same file.
        -->
        <service
            android:name="com.pion.phonecleaner.data.notification.NotificationInterceptorService"
            android:exported="true"
            android:label="@string/channel_hidden_notifications_name"
            android:permission="android.permission.BIND_NOTIFICATION_LISTENER_SERVICE">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.service.notification.NotificationListenerService" />
            </intent-filter>
        </service>

        <!--
          The push entry point (LLM.md §3.6: a manifest component is declared in :data, beside its
          code, and merges into :app).

          exported="false": the only caller is the Firebase Messaging SDK in this same process, and
          the intent-filter below is how it finds the service. Contrast the competitor's out-of-app
          entry, exported="true" with no intent-filter, no android:permission and no caller check,
          forwarding its extras verbatim into a launch (AndroidManifest.xml:501-503,
          mf/EnsileActivity.java:65-72) — any installed app can drive it to any module (LLM.md §7.3).
          There is no exported trampoline here.

          NOTE: nothing sends to this service. There is no backend and none is in scope (owner
          decision 1), and with no google-services.json the default FirebaseApp does not initialise,
          so the SDK never dispatches. The component exists so the boundary is real; see
          data/push/PushMessagingService.kt for where it deliberately stops.

          No POST_NOTIFICATIONS and no RECEIVE_BOOT_COMPLETED are declared on its behalf: both sit
          behind pending owner decisions, and nothing in data/push posts a notification.
        -->
        <service
            android:name="com.pion.phonecleaner.data.push.PushMessagingService"
            android:exported="false">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="com.google.firebase.MESSAGING_EVENT" />
            </intent-filter>
        </service>

    </application>

</manifest>
